            <ArticleTitle>Comparing impact and cost-effectiveness of primary prevention strategies for lipid-lowering.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>243-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Lipid-lowering therapy is costly but effective at reducing coronary heart disease (CHD) risk.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the cost-effectiveness and public health impact of Adult Treatment Panel III (ATP III) guidelines and compare with a range of risk- and age-based alternative strategies.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">The CHD Policy Model, a Markov-type cost-effectiveness model.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">National surveys (1999 to 2004), vital statistics (2000), the Framingham Heart Study (1948 to 2000), other published data, and a direct survey of statin costs (2008).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TARGET POPULATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">U.S. population age 35 to 85 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="TIME HORIZON" NlmCategory="METHODS">2010 to 2040.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PERSPECTIVE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Health care system.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Lowering of low-density lipoprotein cholesterol with HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (statins).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURE" NlmCategory="METHODS">Incremental cost-effectiveness.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS OF BASE-CASE ANALYSIS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Full adherence to ATP III primary prevention guidelines would require starting (9.7 million) or intensifying (1.4 million) statin therapy for 11.1 million adults and would prevent 20,000 myocardial infarctions and 10,000 CHD deaths per year at an annual net cost of $3.6 billion ($42,000/QALY) if low-intensity statins cost $2.11 per pill. The ATP III guidelines would be preferred over alternative strategies if society is willing to pay $50,000/QALY and statins cost $1.54 to $2.21 per pill. At higher statin costs, ATP III is not cost-effective; at lower costs, more liberal statin-prescribing strategies would be preferred; and at costs less than $0.10 per pill, treating all persons with low-density lipoprotein cholesterol levels greater than 3.4 mmol/L (&gt;130 mg/dL) would yield net cost savings.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS OF SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Results are sensitive to the assumptions that LDL cholesterol becomes less important as a risk factor with increasing age and that little disutility results from taking a pill every day.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LIMITATION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Randomized trial evidence for statin effectiveness is not available for all subgroups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The ATP III guidelines are relatively cost-effective and would have a large public health impact if implemented fully in the United States. Alternate strategies may be preferred, however, depending on the cost of statins and how much society is willing to pay for better health outcomes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="FUNDING" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Flight Attendants' Medical Research Institute and the Swanson Family Fund. The Framingham Heart Study and Framingham Offspring Study are conducted and supported by the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
